body:
## What are you doing?

Consider the following code:

```js
const Sequelize = require("sequelize");
const sequelize = new Sequelize("postgres://postgres@localhost:5432/testdb");

const Foo = sequelize.define("foo", { name: Sequelize.STRING }, { timestamps: false });
const Bar = sequelize.define("bar", { name: Sequelize.STRING }, { timestamps: false });
const Baz = sequelize.define("baz", { name: Sequelize.STRING }, { timestamps: false });
Foo.belongsTo(Baz, { foreignKey: 'bazId' });
Foo.hasOne(Bar, { foreignKey: 'fooId' });
Foo.addScope('includeBar', { include: Bar });
Foo.addScope('includeBaz', { include: Baz });

const log = result => console.log(JSON.stringify(result, null, 4));

sequelize.sync()
    .then(() => Baz.create({ name: "TheBaz" }))
    .then(baz => Foo.create({ name: "TheFoo", bazId: baz.id }))
    .then(foo => Bar.create({ name: "TheBar", fooId: foo.id }))
    .then(() => Foo.scope("includeBar", "includeBaz").findAll().then(log))
    .then(() => Foo.scope("includeBaz", "includeBar").findAll().then(log))
    .then(() => Foo.findAll({ include: [Bar, Baz] }).then(log))
    .finally(() => sequelize.close());
```

## What do you expect to happen?

The three `findAll` results should be the same, including both `Bar` and `Baz`.

## What is actually happening?

The first one includes only `Baz`, the second only includes only `Bar`, and the third one works correctly.

## Notes

This is a regression. The code above works correctly until `v5.0.0-beta.13`, and doesn't work correctly (as shown above) starting from `v5.0.0-beta.14`.

Interestingly, the `v5.0.0-beta.14` is exactly the version in which the solution to my previous issue #9087 was released, also involving merging includes. Unfortunately, the simple case above was missed by me and everyone else.

## Quick workaround

Changing the code from

```js
Foo.addScope('includeBar', { include: Bar });
Foo.addScope('includeBaz', { include: Baz });
```

to

```js
Foo.addScope('includeBar', { include: [ Bar ] });
Foo.addScope('includeBaz', { include: [ Baz ] });
```

fixes the problem.

This is probably some mistake in `_conformOptions` or `_conformInclude` at `lib/model.js`. Pinging @u9r52sld who wrote the PR solving my issue (#9735). This is probably an easy fix. My suggestion: convert every include to the "full" format, i.e., `[{ model: Foo }]`.
